  "protocolRevision": "PROTOCOL REVISION (user-approved; record it verbatim in the round's receipts):\n- Each Kotoba workload w runs n_w = LSF_w \u00b7 8192 \u00b7 m_w iterations, with m_w \u2208 {1,2,4,8,16}. Reported ratio_w = (4/m_w) \u00b7 K_ns / C_ns. The full-19 GM is over those ratios.\n- m_w is fixed BEFORE transfer from local data only, never adjusted after any Joseph timing. Write it into a pre-registration receipt (hashed) in s3. Rule: m_w = the smallest power of two such that the projected Joseph interval \u2265 1.0 s, using the conservative Joseph/local ratio 0.33 (local_elapsed \u00b7 m_w \u00b7 0.33 \u2265 1.0 s), subject to fuel: (benchmarkFuel + verifyFuel)(n_w) must stay well under 2^35 (\u2264 ~0.9 \u00b7 2^35). If fuel binds, take the largest feasible m_w and report the projection.\n- Unchanged: the C arm (GSF 32768, frozen round65 binaries); the 0.5 s floor; fuel 2^35; the trusted loader; warm1/sample1; \u22643 CPU-only retries; first qualified; no subset GM.\n- Arena caps (KEXE_* env): raise only where the new n needs it (e.g. qrduino KEXE_PAIRS 65536 vs 40960\u00b7m pairs, slre pairs, aha vectors), and record every change.\n- Expected exactness values (benchmarkFuel, verifyFuel, result, the 17-counter arena line) must be re-derived for each new n from a TRUSTED reference, never from the candidate m92 itself. Use the same method that produced expected-original-accounting.json, or something at least as independent. If fuel/arena are exactly linear in n, show it from \u22652 reference n values per workload. Tell me what reference you used.\n- Add the local short-interval screen to s3: refuse to transfer if any workload's projected Joseph interval at its m_w is < 0.75 s.\n\nPROTOCOL AMENDMENT 1 (coordinator decision, before any pre-registration; replaces the local-wall-time projection, the 0.33 ratio and the 0.75 s screen):\n1) LOCAL IDLE GATE: drop it for pre-registration. This machine carries persistent unrelated load (java pid 73536, hermes, Codex, node), which I'm not allowed to kill, so \u226570% idle may never happen. Local wall time under load is meaningless anyway. Use a deterministic, load-independent projection instead.\nP_w(m) = I_w \u00b7 m / R_w, where:\n- I_w = the candidate's instruction count for w at m=1 (local, deterministic: the instr= values your s3/validate runs record);\n- R_w = the FASTEST Joseph instructions-per-second observed for w in any previous round (m43 17 workloads, m87 6 incl. matmult's short attempt). Use the instruction count of that exact prior image (recount locally if needed);\n- if w has no prior Joseph data, R_w = 30e9 instr/s.\nFastest R gives the shortest projection, so the rule is conservative for the floor. Calibration comes only from earlier rounds, never from this round, so pre-registration stays clean. Record the R_w table and its sources in the pre-registration receipt.\n2) SELECTION AND SCREEN (replaces my 0.33-ratio and 0.75 s rules):\n- m_w = the smallest power of two in {1,2,4,8,16} with P_w(m_w) \u2265 1.0 s, capped by fuel \u2264 0.9\u00b72^35.\n- Retime rule: if (image, n_w) was already timed on Joseph (aha-mont64, crc32 and matmult-int at m=1 in m87), take the next feasible power of two instead. That makes aha-mont64 m \u2265 2 and needs its KEXE_VECTORS/VECTOR_ITEMS caps doubled; record it.\n- Screen: refuse transfer if any P_w(m_w) < 0.6 s.\n- Example: matmult-int at m=8 projects 1.49e9\u00b78/12.4e9 \u2248 0.96 s using its identical m87 image's own Joseph rate.\n\nPROTOCOL AMENDMENT 2 (coordinator decision, before any m93 pre-registration and before any Joseph timing of this round):\nR_w DECISION: NARROW, i.e. m43 accepted (17) + m87 executed (6). That is what my parenthetical and example specified; the broad reading was my wording's fault, not yours.\n- Record it as Amendment 2, verbatim, and keep the broad pre-registration 91e3daad\u2026 on record as superseded. No Joseph timing has happened since, so m_w is still fixed from local data only.\n- Workloads with no narrow-set data use R = 30e9.\n",
